messages:
Jae Min: Quick head-scratcher in the Kotlin sample: intermittent auth-refresh storms on Android. App hits a 401, our interceptor calls a suspend refresh function, and suddenly multiple requests kick off parallel refreshes and overload auth endpoint.
Olga Ivanova: is the refresh protected by a mutex/singleflight? we had a similar issue with coroutines + retrofit before.
Jae Min: no, we call AuthManager.refreshIfNeeded() from an interceptor which uses runBlocking { suspendRefresh() } — naive but worked in tests. On device it sometimes blocks threads in OkHttp dispatcher and other requests time out then trigger more refreshes.
Priya Nair: mixing blocking calls and coroutines inside interceptors is risky. Interceptor threads are limited by OkHttp dispatcher and can deadlock or starve. Can you paste the snippet?
Jae Min: snippet:
```
class AuthInterceptor : Interceptor {
  override fun intercept(chain: Interceptor.Chain): Response {
    val req = chain.request()
    if (AuthManager.isExpired()) {
      // oops: calling suspend function on IO thread
      runBlocking { AuthManager.refreshTokenSuspend() }
    }
    return chain.proceed(req.newBuilder().header("Authorization","Bearer ${AuthManager.token}").build())
  }
}
```
Olga Ivanova: yep that will block OkHttp worker threads if refresh uses Retrofit (which uses same client by default) -> thread pool exhaustion -> waterfall of pending calls -> more 401s.
Priya Nair: better patterns: (1) use an Authenticator (OkHttp Authenticator runs on a separate thread and is designed for refresh+retry) or (2) implement a singleflight/mutex so only one refresh occurs and others await the result asynchronously (without blocking OkHttp threads).
Jae Min: switching to Authenticator might be cleaner since it gives a route to clone calls for retry. But we also need to avoid calling suspend code from interceptors.
Olga Ivanova: exactly. If you must use coroutines, use an external non-blocking refresh mechanism: a SharedFlow / Mutex + coroutine scope that refreshes and broadcasts completion. Interceptor should check a volatile token and if refresh in-flight, wait with a short backoff or fail-fast.
Priya Nair: also consider using call.clone() for retries handled outside interceptor. Example Authenticator pattern we use in sample:
```
class TokenAuthenticator : Authenticator {
  override fun authenticate(route: Route?, response: Response): Request? {
    synchronized(this) {
      if (!isTokenStale(response)) return null
      val newToken = blockingRefreshToken() // use a blocking HTTP client separate from main OkHttp if needed
      return response.request.newBuilder().header("Authorization","Bearer $newToken").build()
    }
  }
}
```
Jae Min: good point. For the sample I will: 1) remove runBlocking from interceptor, 2) introduce a singleflight refresh util that uses CoroutineMutex + Deferred so multiple callers await one refresh, 3) move retry logic to an Authenticator or show both patterns in docs.
ci-bot: created ticket RD-INF-10234: "android-kotlin: interceptor blocking + coroutine refresh -> auth refresh storms; update sample to use singleflight or Authenticator"
Olga Ivanova: also add diagnostics: telemetry event when refresh concurrency > 1 and log warning: "multiple concurrent refreshes detected". That will help measure customer impact.
Priya Nair: test plan: emulator with MockAuth that delays refresh, spawn 10 concurrent calls that will 401 and assert only one real refresh hits the auth server when singleflight enabled. Also assert no OkHttp thread starvation.
Jae Min: will send a PR updating sample, adding singleflight util, moving long-running network calls off OkHttp dispatcher, and a doc note: "Avoid runBlocking in interceptors; prefer Authenticator or non-blocking singleflight refresh." :thumbsup:
